WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.530
Merchant Sales-Based Financing.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.420
A business needs money
to repair its equipment.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.910
A lender advances the money,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:12.200
and the business repays it with
a percentage of its daily sales.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.420
In this simulation, the business
receives $146,600.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.730
With the fixed financing charge,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.380
it agrees to repay just over $184,000.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.940
The agreed share is just under 10%
of eligible sales.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.810
On day 1, that comes to about $2,400.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.900
On day 3, the business
has no eligible sales.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.930
Its payment is zero,
so its balance stays the same.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.190
On day 111, the share of that day’s sales

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.210
comes to about $3,030.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.350
How much does the business still owe?

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.220
Only $1,486.49.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.480
That’s the final payment,
and the balance reaches zero.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.620
Try the calculator.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.050
At 10%, $1,000 in sales
gives a $100 payment.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.390
What happens if the business
only owes $60?

